            "text": "There is a particular kind of quiet that belongs to a city before sunrise. Not silence: refrigerators hum behind locked caf\u00e9 doors, a bus exhales at an empty stop, and somewhere a delivery driver argues gently with a stubborn gate. The city is awake, but it has not introduced itself.\n\nAt this hour, ordinary things seem briefly unclaimed. A bench is not a place to wait; it is simply wood holding the cold. A puddle becomes a small, unreliable sky. Even the traffic lights appear contemplative, offering their instructions to nobody with an admirable commitment to the possibility of company.\n\nI like imagining that every day begins with this small rehearsal. The baker tests the oven. The street sweeper draws patient circles around yesterday. Someone on a balcony waters a plant that has survived three apartments and one terrible breakup. Nothing spectacular happens, which is precisely what makes it precious.\n\nWe are often told to search for meaning in extraordinary moments: departures, declarations, victories, storms. But most of a life is made from smaller materials. Clean socks. A familiar staircase. The sound of someone chopping onions in another room. A message that says nothing more than, \u201cDid you get home?\u201d\n\nEventually, the first caf\u00e9 opens. Chairs scrape the pavement, and a spoon rings against a cup. The spell does not break; it merely changes shape. What was quiet becomes conversation. What was waiting becomes movement. Above the rooftops, the light arrives without applause, and everyone begins again, ready or not."
